  Let me write it out.

  For even n=2m: pairs (i, 2m-1-i) for i=0..m-1, these are m disjoint pairs covering all positions.
  For odd n=2m+1: pairs i=0..m-1 plus center m. Center maps to itself.

  Lower bound detail: For a fixed i<j, define final string t (palindrome). t[i]=t[j]. If s[i]≠s[j], then t[i]≠s[i] or t[j]≠s[j] (if both equal to original, then t[i]=s[i]≠s[j]=t[j], contradiction). So at least one operation touches position i or j. Since pairs are disjoint, operations needed ≥ number of mismatched pairs. Note also an operation touches exactly one position, so it can "serve" at most one pair.

  Construction detail: For each mismatched pair, change position j to s[i] (one operation). Matched pairs: no ops. Then define t; for even n, for every i in [0, m-1], t[i]=t[2m-1-i]: if originally matched, unchanged equal; if mismatched, we set t[j]=s[i]=t[i]. So palindrome. For odd n, additionally center unchanged, t[m]=s[m]=t[m] trivially since 2m-m = m. Hence palindrome. Total ops = k, matching lower bound. Also optimality: 0 ops iff palindrome.

  Also mention that choosing to change position i instead of j also works; either is fine.

  Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space for the string (needed to store input); extra O(1).

